[Event "Chapter 1: Jaenisch Gambit Main Lines / Study 1: White Plays 4.d3 / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/0aa60d8d-e4ca-448a-808c-630cb0656846/chapters/4ef816ae-a604-4940-98b7-4eb78d0db82b/studies/d75df114-aee8-47f7-bd22-0a181b6b1c01/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to the Jaenisch Gambit! This chapter will deal with the main lines within this gambit. Hence why the positions we'll analyze in this study happen after 1.e4 e5 2.Nf3 Nc6 3.Bb5 f5, and then the very logical 4.d3. White opens with the king's pawn. We'll reply with ...e5.} 1...e5 2.Nf3 {The knight attacks e5. We'll defend the pawn with Nc6.} 2...Nc6 3.Bb5 {And White plays the Ruy Lopez, attacking the defender of e5. With ...f5, we offer up a pawn! This is our gambit! White has many plausible responses. In this study we'll analyze 4.d3.} 3...f5 4.d3 {White chooses to defend e4 with another pawn. Very logical, and the kind of move an unprepared opponent could well find. This move is played 26% of the time, which is around 450,000 games. We'll capture on e4.} 4...fxe4 5.dxe4 {White recaptures. Now we can develop and attack e4.} 5...Nf6 6.Bg5 {Bg5 pins the attacker of e4. Expect to see it 12% of the time. We can temporarily ignore the pin and develop our bishop aiming at f2.} 6...Bc5 7.Nc3 {White develops to c3. Now that our bishop is outside of the pawns we'll play d6.} 7...d6 8.Nd5 {Nd5 applies pressure on our pinned piece. But here we have a tactical trick, taking on f2 with check!} 8...Bxf2+ 9.Kxf2 {White captures. And we can capture on e4 with check, unpinning and attacking the g5-bishop!} 9...Nxe4+ 10.Kg1 {The king retreats. We'll take the bishop that was attacking us.} 10...Nxg5 11.Nxg5 {White recaptures. And we'll recapture, too. We're now two pawns up, but c7 is loose.} 11...Qxg5 12.h4 {White attacks our queen, and makes room for their king. The immediate Nc7+ would have ended up losing to a future Qe3+ and Rf8+. We'll play our queen to g3, where we threaten a perpetual check if White moves the knight.} 12...Qg3 13.Nxc7+ {It seems we have allowed a fork, but we just move our king to defend the d6-pawn, and...} 13...Ke7 14.Nxa8 {White takes our rook.} 14...Qe3+ {And we have perpetual checks via Qe3+ and Qf4+. White cannot step onto the f-file because we then bring our rook to f8 and win the game.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Jaenisch Gambit Main Lines / Study 1: White Plays 4.d3 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/0aa60d8d-e4ca-448a-808c-630cb0656846/chapters/4ef816ae-a604-4940-98b7-4eb78d0db82b/studies/d75df114-aee8-47f7-bd22-0a181b6b1c01/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to the Jaenisch Gambit! This chapter will deal with the main lines within this gambit. Hence why the positions we'll analyze in this study happen after 1.e4 e5 2.Nf3 Nc6 3.Bb5 f5, and then the very logical 4.d3. White opens with the king's pawn. We'll reply with ...e5.} 1...e5 2.Nf3 {The knight attacks e5. We'll defend the pawn with Nc6.} 2...Nc6 3.Bb5 {And White plays the Ruy Lopez, attacking the defender of e5. With ...f5, we offer up a pawn! This is our gambit! White has many plausible responses. In this study we'll analyze 4.d3.} 3...f5 4.d3 {White chooses to defend e4 with another pawn. Very logical, and the kind of move an unprepared opponent could well find. This move is played 26% of the time, which is around 450,000 games. We'll capture on e4.} 4...fxe4 5.dxe4 {White recaptures. Now we can develop and attack e4.} 5...Nf6 6.O-O {White here can castle, as we're not prepared to take on e4 because of our lack of development. And they will 28% of the time. We can now develop our bishop outside of the pawn chain before we play d6.} 6...Bc5 7.Bxc6 {Bxc6 damaging our structure is a valid continuation. Your opponents will remove the defender of the e5-pawn here 24% of the time. We'll capture towards the center.} 7...bxc6 8.Nxe5 {White grabs a pawn! Oh no! They can do this? We'll just castle, and prioritize our development. We are going to play gambit style.} 8...O-O 9.Nc3 {White develops, protecting e4. Development is key. We will play to open up our light-squared bishop.} 9...d6 10.Nxc6 {White gobbles up a pawn!} 10...Qe8 {Qe8 gives us good compensation, as we attack the knight, plus we have additional pressure on e4 (which we'll win) and we'll have pressure on f2 as well.} *

