1.e4 {Welcome to the Jaenisch Gambit! This chapter will deal with the main lines within this gambit. Hence why the positions we'll analyze in this study happen after 1.e4 e5 2.Nf3 Nc6 3.Bb5 f5, and then the very logical 4.d3. White opens with the king's pawn. We'll reply with ...e5.} 1...e5 2.Nf3 {The knight attacks e5. We'll defend the pawn with Nc6.} 2...Nc6 3.Bb5 {And White plays the Ruy Lopez, attacking the defender of e5. With ...f5, we offer up a pawn! This is our gambit! White has many plausible responses. In this study we'll analyze 4.d3.} 3...f5 4.d3 {White chooses to defend e4 with another pawn. Very logical, and the kind of move an unprepared opponent could well find. This move is played 26% of the time, which is around 450,000 games. We'll capture on e4.} 4...fxe4 5.dxe4 {White recaptures. Now we can develop and attack e4.} 5...Nf6 6.O-O {White here can castle, as we're not prepared to take on e4 because of our lack of development. And they will 28% of the time. We can now develop our bishop outside of the pawn chain before we play d6.} 6...Bc5 7.Bxc6 {Bxc6 damaging our structure is a valid continuation. Your opponents will remove the defender of the e5-pawn here 24% of the time. We'll capture towards the center.} 7...bxc6 8.Nxe5 {White grabs a pawn! Oh no! They can do this? We'll just castle, and prioritize our development. We are going to play gambit style.} 8...O-O 9.Nc3 {White develops, protecting e4. Development is key. We will play to open up our light-squared bishop.} 9...d6 10.Nd3 {White goes back to d3 where it attacks our bishop. We move away from danger, putting pressure on the white center.} 10...Bd4 11.Qe1 {White defends e4.} 11...Qe8 {We apply pressure on e4. We have open lines and the bishop pair - good compensation for the pawn.} *

