[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/0aa60d8d-e4ca-448a-808c-630cb0656846/chapters/4ef816ae-a604-4940-98b7-4eb78d0db82b/studies/d75df114-aee8-47f7-bd22-0a181b6b1c01/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to the Jaenisch Gambit! This chapter will deal with the main lines within this gambit. Hence why the positions we'll analyze in this study happen after 1.e4 e5 2.Nf3 Nc6 3.Bb5 f5, and then the very logical 4.d3. White opens with the king's pawn. We'll reply with ...e5.} 1...e5 2.Nf3 {The knight attacks e5. We'll defend the pawn with Nc6.} 2...Nc6 3.Bb5 {And White plays the Ruy Lopez, attacking the defender of e5. With ...f5, we offer up a pawn! This is our gambit! White has many plausible responses. In this study we'll analyze 4.d3.} 3...f5 4.d3 {White chooses to defend e4 with another pawn. Very logical, and the kind of move an unprepared opponent could well find. This move is played 26% of the time, which is around 450,000 games. We'll capture on e4.} 4...fxe4 5.dxe4 {White recaptures. Now we can develop and attack e4.} 5...Nf6 6.Nc3 {Nc3 defends the pawn on e4. It's the most popular and natural-looking move, played 36% of the time. We can pin the knight with Bb4!} 6...Bb4 7.O-O {White unpins by castling. But we'll capture, damaging the white pawn structure.} 7...Bxc3 8.bxc3 {White recaptures. And we get castled ourselves.} 8...O-O 9.Bxc6 {White can also take, removing the defender of e5. We'll capture towards the center.} 9...bxc6 10.Nxe5 {If the knight takes, we can...} 10...Qe8 {Attack the knight and x-ray e4 behind it, ensuring we'll regain our pawn.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Jaenisch Gambit Main Lines / Study 1: White Plays 4.d3 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/0aa60d8d-e4ca-448a-808c-630cb0656846/chapters/4ef816ae-a604-4940-98b7-4eb78d0db82b/studies/d75df114-aee8-47f7-bd22-0a181b6b1c01/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to the Jaenisch Gambit! This chapter will deal with the main lines within this gambit. Hence why the positions we'll analyze in this study happen after 1.e4 e5 2.Nf3 Nc6 3.Bb5 f5, and then the very logical 4.d3. White opens with the king's pawn. We'll reply with ...e5.} 1...e5 2.Nf3 {The knight attacks e5. We'll defend the pawn with Nc6.} 2...Nc6 3.Bb5 {And White plays the Ruy Lopez, attacking the defender of e5. With ...f5, we offer up a pawn! This is our gambit! White has many plausible responses. In this study we'll analyze 4.d3.} 3...f5 4.d3 {White chooses to defend e4 with another pawn. Very logical, and the kind of move an unprepared opponent could well find. This move is played 26% of the time, which is around 450,000 games. We'll capture on e4.} 4...fxe4 5.dxe4 {White recaptures. Now we can develop and attack e4.} 5...Nf6 6.O-O {White here can castle, as we're not prepared to take on e4 because of our lack of development. And they will 28% of the time. We can now develop our bishop outside of the pawn chain before we play d6.} 6...Bc5 7.Nc3 {White can choose to develop a piece defending e4. This is the most common move, played 30% of the time. We'll reply with ...d6. It's worth noting that White will occasionally play Qd3 here instead of Nc3. Here, too, we should play d6, and protect our bishop against any sneaky forks with Qc4.} 7...d6 8.Bg5 {White pins us. But we can now castle when our rook supports the knight on f6.} 8...O-O 9.Nd5 {White pressurizes f6.} 9...Kh8 {We get our king off of the dangerous diagonal. To be clear: we are not afraid of Nxf6 gxf6. We'd like the g-file to open up for our rook! The d4-square, if available, would also be a good square for our c6-knight.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Jaenisch Gambit Main Lines / Study 1: White Plays 4.d3 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/0aa60d8d-e4ca-448a-808c-630cb0656846/chapters/4ef816ae-a604-4940-98b7-4eb78d0db82b/studies/d75df114-aee8-47f7-bd22-0a181b6b1c01/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to the Jaenisch Gambit! This chapter will deal with the main lines within this gambit. Hence why the positions we'll analyze in this study happen after 1.e4 e5 2.Nf3 Nc6 3.Bb5 f5, and then the very logical 4.d3. White opens with the king's pawn. We'll reply with ...e5.} 1...e5 2.Nf3 {The knight attacks e5. We'll defend the pawn with Nc6.} 2...Nc6 3.Bb5 {And White plays the Ruy Lopez, attacking the defender of e5. With ...f5, we offer up a pawn! This is our gambit! White has many plausible responses. In this study we'll analyze 4.d3.} 3...f5 4.d3 {White chooses to defend e4 with another pawn. Very logical, and the kind of move an unprepared opponent could well find. This move is played 26% of the time, which is around 450,000 games. We'll capture on e4.} 4...fxe4 5.dxe4 {White recaptures. Now we can develop and attack e4.} 5...Nf6 6.Nc3 {Nc3 defends the pawn on e4. It's the most popular and natural-looking move, played 36% of the time. We can pin the knight with Bb4!} 6...Bb4 7.O-O {White unpins by castling. But we'll capture, damaging the white pawn structure.} 7...Bxc3 8.bxc3 {White recaptures. And we get castled ourselves.} 8...O-O 9.Bg5 {White can develop, pinning us.} 9...d6 {Now we can start placing our pawns on dark squares, given that we've traded our dark-squared bishop. We have the superior pawn structure. We want to play ...Qe8, ...Be6, and safety moves like ...Kh8. If Bc4 at some point, we don't mind a trade of bishops and to maneuver the knight with ...Nd8. Our queen from e8 can then go to the kingside.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Jaenisch Gambit Main Lines / Study 2: White Plays 4.Bxc6 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/0aa60d8d-e4ca-448a-808c-630cb0656846/chapters/4ef816ae-a604-4940-98b7-4eb78d0db82b/studies/b4e3430f-7b77-44f2-834c-bf818398d49a/lines"]

1.e4 {Since the Jaenisch arises out of the Ruy Lopez, we must ask the question: what if White chooses to take our knight on c6 with the bishop? This study aims to answer that question by giving you some dynamic options against this as well. White opens with the king's pawn. We'll reply with ...e5.} 1...e5 2.Nf3 {The knight attacks e5. We'll defend the pawn with Nc6.} 2...Nc6 3.Bb5 {And White plays the Ruy Lopez, attacking the defender of e5. With ...f5, the Jaenisch Gambit, we offer up a pawn! In this study we'll analyze 4.Bxc6.} 3...f5 4.Bxc6 {White eliminates our knight, removing a supporter of our center. This is the most popular move and it's played in almost 500,000 games, which is 28%. We'll take away from the center, opening up our bishop and queen.} 4...dxc6 5.Nxe5 {White takes the undefended pawn. This happens in 48% of games. This is the main point behind Bxc6. We'll fork the knight and pawn!} 5...Qd4 6.Nf3 {White returns the knight, attacking our queen. And now we regain the pawn. We have acquired the bishop pair.} 6...Qxe4+ 7.Qe2 {Play can continue with White blocking, offering a queen trade. We'll develop the bishop with Bd7. We are happy for White to exchange queens and bring our pawn to a dominant square.} 7...Bd7 8.d3 {White presses for the exchange of queens, but not on e4. We'll take on e2.} 8...Qxe2+ 9.Kxe2 {White recaptures.} 9...O-O-O {And we castle long, with an active position.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Jaenisch Gambit Main Lines / Study 2: White Plays 4.Bxc6 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/0aa60d8d-e4ca-448a-808c-630cb0656846/chapters/4ef816ae-a604-4940-98b7-4eb78d0db82b/studies/b4e3430f-7b77-44f2-834c-bf818398d49a/lines"]

1.e4 {Since the Jaenisch arises out of the Ruy Lopez, we must ask the question: what if White chooses to take our knight on c6 with the bishop? This study aims to answer that question by giving you some dynamic options against this as well. White opens with the king's pawn. We'll reply with ...e5.} 1...e5 2.Nf3 {The knight attacks e5. We'll defend the pawn with Nc6.} 2...Nc6 3.Bb5 {And White plays the Ruy Lopez, attacking the defender of e5. With ...f5, the Jaenisch Gambit, we offer up a pawn! In this study we'll analyze 4.Bxc6.} 3...f5 4.Bxc6 {White eliminates our knight, removing a supporter of our center. This is the most popular move and it's played in almost 500,000 games, which is 28%. We'll take away from the center, opening up our bishop and queen.} 4...dxc6 5.Nc3 {White defends e4 with a knight. This "less tactical" approach is chosen 5% of the time. We'll apply pressure to e4.} 5...Nf6 6.Qe2 {White defends e4. Let's just trade that central pawn.} 6...fxe4 7.Nxe4 {White recaptures. We'll develop our light-squared bishop and pin the f3-knight.} 7...Bg4 8.h3 {White challenges our bishop. We'll capture the knight.} 8...Bxf3 9.Qxf3 {The queen recaptures. We'll trade on e4.} 9...Nxe4 10.Qxe4 {White recaptures.} 10...Qd5 {And we propose yet another trade. The position is equal.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Jaenisch Gambit Main Lines / Study 3: White Plays 4.exf5 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/0aa60d8d-e4ca-448a-808c-630cb0656846/chapters/4ef816ae-a604-4940-98b7-4eb78d0db82b/studies/8fa81ba9-5136-47f9-aa34-651a486928a5/lines"]

1.e4 {Let's delve deep into the final study of the chapter. White can choose to continue by just taking the pawn offered to them. In this case, we have everything we want! The central control is ours. After 1.e4 e5 2.Nf3 Nc6 3.Bb5 f5 4.exf5, we'll have 4...e4 attacking the knight, and a fun game for sure! Let's start with 1.e4 and our reply of ...e5!} 1...e5 2.Nf3 {The knight attacks e5. We'll defend the pawn with Nc6.} 2...Nc6 3.Bb5 {And White plays the Ruy Lopez, attacking the defender of e5. With ...f5, we offer up a pawn! This is our gambit! White has many plausible responses. In this study we'll analyze what happens if White captures on f5.} 3...f5 4.exf5 {If White chooses to take, they play into our hand! And they will do so 25% of the time, which is about 440,000 games. Now with ...e4 we will control the center and bully them!} 4...e4 5.Qe2 {The queen intervenes, attacking our pawn and pinning it to our king! Expect to see this move a third of the time, 33%. We'll break the pin and defend e4 by playing Qe7.} 5...Qe7 6.Bxc6 {White removes our knight from c6. This was the idea of 3. Bb5, so they might as well follow through. Let's recapture towards the center. The f3-knight remains en prise!} 6...bxc6 7.Nd4 {The white knight comes forward, trying to defend the pawn they grabbed on f5. We need to address the possibility of Qh5+, so we'll develop our knight and defend e4 in the process. Our control of the center is greater than White's.} 7...Nf6 8.O-O {After castling, we need to bear in mind that White will bring the rook to e1. We'll attack the defender of f5 by playing Qe5.} 8...Qe5 9.Nf3 {White takes advantage of the pin! But they do not stop us from capturing the pawn on f5.} 9...Qxf5 10.Re1 {The rook comes to the e-file. Now with Kf7, we get out of the pin and threaten the knight on f3.} 10...Kf7 11.Nh4 {The knight can gain a tempo on us. But we can move back to e5, with an eye on h2.} 11...Qe5 12.d3 {White can try the very natural d3, seeing that our e-pawn is pinned. We'll develop our bishop, setting up a battery attacking h2!} 12...Bd6 13.g3 {After g3, our rook can enter the game.} 13...Re8 {And now our queen is sufficiently defended and our defence of e4 is great! A good plan for the near future would be to get the bishop to c5 since White has played g3, and then play for ...d5. If White attacks our queen with Bf4, we can retreat it to e7.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Jaenisch Gambit Main Lines / Study 3: White Plays 4.exf5 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/0aa60d8d-e4ca-448a-808c-630cb0656846/chapters/4ef816ae-a604-4940-98b7-4eb78d0db82b/studies/8fa81ba9-5136-47f9-aa34-651a486928a5/lines"]

1.e4 {Let's delve deep into the final study of the chapter. White can choose to continue by just taking the pawn offered to them. In this case, we have everything we want! The central control is ours. After 1.e4 e5 2.Nf3 Nc6 3.Bb5 f5 4.exf5, we'll have 4...e4 attacking the knight, and a fun game for sure! Let's start with 1.e4 and our reply of ...e5!} 1...e5 2.Nf3 {The knight attacks e5. We'll defend the pawn with Nc6.} 2...Nc6 3.Bb5 {And White plays the Ruy Lopez, attacking the defender of e5. With ...f5, we offer up a pawn! This is our gambit! White has many plausible responses. In this study we'll analyze what happens if White captures on f5.} 3...f5 4.exf5 {If White chooses to take, they play into our hand! And they will do so 25% of the time, which is about 440,000 games. Now with ...e4 we will control the center and bully them!} 4...e4 5.Bxc6 {White has a piece under attack. They thus choose to exchange! But this is the wrong approach. And you'll face this wrong approach 46% of the time! We'll capture away from the center, opening up our queen and light-squared bishop. This latter piece in particular attacks f5!} 5...dxc6 6.Qe2 {White pins the e-pawn. We'll take on f5 and defend e4 with our bishop.} 6...Bxf5 7.d3 {With d3, White increases pressure on the pinned pawn.} 7...Qe7 {Thus we defend it once more, breaking the pin. It seems we offered a gambit in the opening, but we're still equal, and we have the bishop pair!} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Jaenisch Gambit Sidelines / Study 1: White Plays 4.Nc3 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/0aa60d8d-e4ca-448a-808c-630cb0656846/chapters/08487333-b01d-45ad-9441-10369ea04ed5/studies/bdf7eeb8-2eaf-4a66-bd75-c5ff6831c3ad/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to the first study of the sidelines chapter! We'll study positions after 1.e4 e5 2.Nf3 Nc6 3.Bb5 f5 4.Nc3. Defending the e-pawn with the knight is actually considered a mainline, so let's pay extra attention and make sure to learn the moves! White opens with the king's pawn. We'll reply with ...e5.} 1...e5 2.Nf3 {The knight attacks e5. We'll defend the pawn with Nc6.} 2...Nc6 3.Bb5 {And White plays the Ruy Lopez, attacking the defender of e5. With ...f5, we enter our gambit! White has many plausible responses. In this study we'll analyze how to respond when White plays 4.Nc3.} 3...f5 4.Nc3 {White defends e4 with the knight. This is the fourth most popular move, played 11% of the time, which is almost 200,000 games. We'll capture on e4.} 4...fxe4 5.Nxe4 {The knight recaptures. We'll develop our king's knight and propose a trade.} 5...Nf6 6.Nxf6+ {White agrees 37% of the time. We'll recapture with the queen.} 6...Qxf6 7.Qe2 {White applies pressure to e5. Let's prepare castling by playing Be7. White can win a pawn, but if they do so we will have compensation.} 7...Be7 8.Bxc6 {White takes the defender of e5. We'll capture towards the center.} 8...bxc6 9.Qxe5 {White grabs the pawn in the center. Let's go for quick development with d6.} 9...d6 10.Qxf6 {White accepts the trade. We'll recapture, bringing our bishop to an active post on f6.} 10...Bxf6 11.d3 {White opens up their bishop. We'll simply castle into safety.} 11...O-O 12.Rb1 {White has to waste a move like this, because otherwise their bishop was tied to the b-pawn. We'll give ourselves the option of doubling White's pawns.} 12...Bg4 13.Be3 {White develops.} 13...Rab8 {Our rook enters the semi-open file where it threatens b2. In the future, we might capture White's knight on f3 and double their pawns. Moves like ...d5 and ...Re8 come to mind, as well.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Jaenisch Gambit Sidelines / Study 1: White Plays 4.Nc3 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/0aa60d8d-e4ca-448a-808c-630cb0656846/chapters/08487333-b01d-45ad-9441-10369ea04ed5/studies/bdf7eeb8-2eaf-4a66-bd75-c5ff6831c3ad/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to the first study of the sidelines chapter! We'll study positions after 1.e4 e5 2.Nf3 Nc6 3.Bb5 f5 4.Nc3. Defending the e-pawn with the knight is actually considered a mainline, so let's pay extra attention and make sure to learn the moves! White opens with the king's pawn. We'll reply with ...e5.} 1...e5 2.Nf3 {The knight attacks e5. We'll defend the pawn with Nc6.} 2...Nc6 3.Bb5 {And White plays the Ruy Lopez, attacking the defender of e5. With ...f5, we enter our gambit! White has many plausible responses. In this study we'll analyze how to respond when White plays 4.Nc3.} 3...f5 4.Nc3 {White defends e4 with the knight. This is the fourth most popular move, played 11% of the time, which is almost 200,000 games. We'll capture on e4.} 4...fxe4 5.Nxe4 {The knight recaptures. We'll develop our king's knight and propose a trade.} 5...Nf6 6.Nxf6+ {White agrees 37% of the time. We'll recapture with the queen.} 6...Qxf6 7.Qe2 {White applies pressure to e5. Let's prepare castling by playing Be7. White can win a pawn, but if they do so we will have compensation.} 7...Be7 8.Bxc6 {White takes the defender of e5. We'll capture towards the center.} 8...bxc6 9.d4 {Another possibility is d4, when White adds yet another attacker on e5. Here we should sidestep and attack g2.} 9...Qg6 10.O-O {White castles, defending g2. We'll play d6 and open up our light-squared bishop.} 10...d6 11.dxe5 {White takes the coveted e5 pawn. But we'll play actively and castle, with ideas of Bh3 or Bg4.} 11...O-O 12.exd6 {If White takes once more...} 12...Bh3 {We'll attack g2!} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Jaenisch Gambit Sidelines / Study 1: White Plays 4.Nc3 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/0aa60d8d-e4ca-448a-808c-630cb0656846/chapters/08487333-b01d-45ad-9441-10369ea04ed5/studies/bdf7eeb8-2eaf-4a66-bd75-c5ff6831c3ad/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to the first study of the sidelines chapter! We'll study positions after 1.e4 e5 2.Nf3 Nc6 3.Bb5 f5 4.Nc3. Defending the e-pawn with the knight is actually considered a mainline, so let's pay extra attention and make sure to learn the moves! White opens with the king's pawn. We'll reply with ...e5.} 1...e5 2.Nf3 {The knight attacks e5. We'll defend the pawn with Nc6.} 2...Nc6 3.Bb5 {And White plays the Ruy Lopez, attacking the defender of e5. With ...f5, we enter our gambit! White has many plausible responses. In this study we'll analyze how to respond when White plays 4.Nc3.} 3...f5 4.Nc3 {White defends e4 with the knight. This is the fourth most popular move, played 11% of the time, which is almost 200,000 games. We'll capture on e4.} 4...fxe4 5.Nxe4 {The knight recaptures. We'll develop our king's knight and propose a trade.} 5...Nf6 6.Nxf6+ {White agrees 37% of the time. We'll recapture with the queen.} 6...Qxf6 7.Qe2 {White applies pressure to e5. Let's prepare castling by playing Be7. White can win a pawn, but if they do so we will have compensation.} 7...Be7 8.Bxc6 {White takes the defender of e5. We'll capture towards the center.} 8...bxc6 9.Nxe5 {White wins the e5 pawn, but we have the bishop pair. We'll switch our queen to the e-file, pinning the knight and posing some questions.} 9...Qe6 10.Nf3 {The knight retreats. White couldn't simply castle because of d6. We'll exchange queens and leave the white king in the center.} 10...Qxe2+ 11.Kxe2 {White recaptures. After ...c5, our bishop is coming out where it'll control the diagonals unopposed.} 11...c5 12.Re1 {The rook steps into the open file. We'll place our bishop actively on b7.} 12...Bb7 13.Kf1 {We are now pinned on the e-file, but we can damage White's pawn structure...} 13...Bxf3 14.gxf3 {The pawn recaptures.} 14...Rf8 {We enter the f-file and begin pressuring f3.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Jaenisch Gambit Sidelines / Study 1: White Plays 4.Nc3 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/0aa60d8d-e4ca-448a-808c-630cb0656846/chapters/08487333-b01d-45ad-9441-10369ea04ed5/studies/bdf7eeb8-2eaf-4a66-bd75-c5ff6831c3ad/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to the first study of the sidelines chapter! We'll study positions after 1.e4 e5 2.Nf3 Nc6 3.Bb5 f5 4.Nc3. Defending the e-pawn with the knight is actually considered a mainline, so let's pay extra attention and make sure to learn the moves! White opens with the king's pawn. We'll reply with ...e5.} 1...e5 2.Nf3 {The knight attacks e5. We'll defend the pawn with Nc6.} 2...Nc6 3.Bb5 {And White plays the Ruy Lopez, attacking the defender of e5. With ...f5, we enter our gambit! White has many plausible responses. In this study we'll analyze how to respond when White plays 4.Nc3.} 3...f5 4.Nc3 {White defends e4 with the knight. This is the fourth most popular move, played 11% of the time, which is almost 200,000 games. We'll capture on e4.} 4...fxe4 5.Nxe4 {The knight recaptures. We'll develop our king's knight and propose a trade.} 5...Nf6 6.Qe2 {White can choose to defend e4 with the queen. They do so 18% of the time. We'll push in the center with ...d5.} 6...d5 7.Nxf6+ {White captures on f6. We'll recapture with the pawn and build a strong center.} 7...gxf6 8.d4 {Attacking the pinned pawn. We'll support the structure and prepare to castle.} 8...Bg7 9.dxe5 {White can actually take it. But we'll castle and play for activity.} 9...O-O 10.Bxc6 {White removes the piece that covered the e5 square. We'll recapture.} 10...bxc6 11.e6 {White pushes their e-pawn so that when we recapture our pawns remain split. We'll play Re8, preparing to recover the pawn.} 11...Re8 12.O-O {White castles. We'll play c5, taking d4 away from the knight.} 12...c5 13.Qb5 {The queen heads to b5 to pressure our pawns from the side. Our bishop can defend from f8.} 13...Bf8 14.Be3 {White increases pressure. But here we can take the pawn already with the rook, threatening Ba6, with a skewer!} 14...Rxe6 15.Rfd1 {The rook puts pressure on our center but most importantly, removes itself from skewer territory! We'll attack the queen and support our center with ...c6.} 15...c6 16.Qa4 {The queen drops back one square.} 16...Qe8 {And we double major pieces on the e-file while adding another defender on c6.} *

