1.e4 {Welcome to the first study of the sidelines chapter! We'll study positions after 1.e4 e5 2.Nf3 Nc6 3.Bb5 f5 4.Nc3. Defending the e-pawn with the knight is actually considered a mainline, so let's pay extra attention and make sure to learn the moves! White opens with the king's pawn. We'll reply with ...e5.} 1...e5 2.Nf3 {The knight attacks e5. We'll defend the pawn with Nc6.} 2...Nc6 3.Bb5 {And White plays the Ruy Lopez, attacking the defender of e5. With ...f5, we enter our gambit! White has many plausible responses. In this study we'll analyze how to respond when White plays 4.Nc3.} 3...f5 4.Nc3 {White defends e4 with the knight. This is the fourth most popular move, played 11% of the time, which is almost 200,000 games. We'll capture on e4.} 4...fxe4 5.Nxe4 {The knight recaptures. We'll develop our king's knight and propose a trade.} 5...Nf6 6.Qe2 {White can choose to defend e4 with the queen. They do so 18% of the time. We'll push in the center with ...d5.} 6...d5 7.Nxf6+ {White captures on f6. We'll recapture with the pawn and build a strong center.} 7...gxf6 8.d4 {Attacking the pinned pawn. We'll support the structure and prepare to castle.} 8...Bg7 9.dxe5 {White can actually take it. But we'll castle and play for activity.} 9...O-O 10.e6 {White doesn't give up the pawn so easily. We'll bring our knight into the center.} 10...Ne5 11.Bf4 {White develops putting pressure on our knight. We'll attack the bishop with ...c6 and cement our structure.} 11...c6 12.Bd3 {White retreats. We'll take the bishop pair!} 12...Nxd3+ 13.Qxd3 {White recaptures.} 13...Bxe6 {And now we can recapture with our bishop on e6, with ideas of repositioning it to g6.} *

