1.e4 {Welcome to the first study of the sidelines chapter! We'll study positions after 1.e4 e5 2.Nf3 Nc6 3.Bb5 f5 4.Nc3. Defending the e-pawn with the knight is actually considered a mainline, so let's pay extra attention and make sure to learn the moves! White opens with the king's pawn. We'll reply with ...e5.} 1...e5 2.Nf3 {The knight attacks e5. We'll defend the pawn with Nc6.} 2...Nc6 3.Bb5 {And White plays the Ruy Lopez, attacking the defender of e5. With ...f5, we enter our gambit! White has many plausible responses. In this study we'll analyze how to respond when White plays 4.Nc3.} 3...f5 4.Nc3 {White defends e4 with the knight. This is the fourth most popular move, played 11% of the time, which is almost 200,000 games. We'll capture on e4.} 4...fxe4 5.Nxe4 {The knight recaptures. We'll develop our king's knight and propose a trade.} 5...Nf6 6.Qe2 {White can choose to defend e4 with the queen. They do so 18% of the time. We'll push in the center with ...d5.} 6...d5 7.Nxf6+ {White captures on f6. We'll recapture with the pawn and build a strong center.} 7...gxf6 8.d4 {Attacking the pinned pawn. We'll support the structure and prepare to castle.} 8...Bg7 9.dxe5 {White can actually take it. But we'll castle and play for activity.} 9...O-O 10.exf6 {White goes for maximum greed!} 10...Qxf6 {We can here just recapture and we're up in development, with open lines for our bishops and heavy pieces.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Jaenisch Gambit Sidelines / Study 2: White Plays 4.d4 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/0aa60d8d-e4ca-448a-808c-630cb0656846/chapters/08487333-b01d-45ad-9441-10369ea04ed5/studies/b55f0438-0a0f-4ef5-a694-f49390549d42/lines"]

1.e4 {In this study we'll go over the position after 1.e4 e5 2.Nf3 Nc6 3.Bb5 f5 4.d4! You can mentally refer to this position as White counterattacks in the center. Let's begin! White opens with the king's pawn. We'll reply with ...e5.} 1...e5 2.Nf3 {The knight attacks e5. We'll defend the pawn with Nc6.} 2...Nc6 3.Bb5 {The Ruy Lopez, threatening the stability of our center! With ...f5, we enter our gambit!} 3...f5 4.d4 {White counters in the center! Notice each pawn in the center can be captured! This tricky move is played 5% of the time, which is around 80,000 games. Best is to capture on e4, destabilizing the knight on f3.} 4...fxe4 5.Nxe5 {White captures one of our pawns and gets out of danger. Expect to see this move by far the most, 74% of the time. Let's take on e5! White would've been willing to take us on c6 either way. Now the bishop on b5 is misplaced, surely.} 5...Nxe5 6.dxe5 {Recapturing. By playing c6, we gain a tempo on the bishop! Additionally, we open up the diagonal so our queen can check on a5, followed by taking on e5!} 6...c6 7.Nc3 {Craziness on the board! The bishop is hanging! But White argues they have counterplay. Only 14% of players will know about this tricky move. We'll take the bishop.} 7...cxb5 8.Nxe4 {White captures our pawn in the center. Careful! There are some lines open to our king! With ...d5, we give up a pawn but facilitate our development.} 8...d5 9.exd6 {En passant! White is obviously a cultured individual. We see an immediate benefit of giving up our d-pawn, and can now develop the knight to f6.} 9...Nf6 10.Bg5 {Another idea is for White to pin our knight, attacking it with two pieces. We'll give a check and unpin with tempo!} 10...Qa5+ 11.Nc3 {The knight blocks. We'll push ...b4 and attack two pieces.} 11...b4 12.Bxf6 {White saves a piece by taking one. We'll recapture on f6.} 12...gxf6 13.Nd5 {The white knight leaps forward, threatening to come to c7. ...b3+ is our best move here.} 13...b3+ 14.c3 {c3 blocks. And now we go for maximum development!} 14...Be6 15.Nc7+ {White checks us. We'll protect the e6-bishop, and hide behind the d-pawn.} 15...Kd7 16.Nxa8 {What if White just grabs the rook?} 16...Qe5+ {This check is very compelling. White loses castling rights. Their rook on h1 will have a hard time developing. Furthermore, a simple ...Bxd6 will open up an attack on the knight and White cannot save it.} *

