[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/0aa60d8d-e4ca-448a-808c-630cb0656846/chapters/08487333-b01d-45ad-9441-10369ea04ed5/studies/b55f0438-0a0f-4ef5-a694-f49390549d42/lines"]

1.e4 {In this study we'll go over the position after 1.e4 e5 2.Nf3 Nc6 3.Bb5 f5 4.d4! You can mentally refer to this position as White counterattacks in the center. Let's begin! White opens with the king's pawn. We'll reply with ...e5.} 1...e5 2.Nf3 {The knight attacks e5. We'll defend the pawn with Nc6.} 2...Nc6 3.Bb5 {The Ruy Lopez, threatening the stability of our center! With ...f5, we enter our gambit!} 3...f5 4.d4 {White counters in the center! Notice each pawn in the center can be captured! This tricky move is played 5% of the time, which is around 80,000 games. Best is to capture on e4, destabilizing the knight on f3.} 4...fxe4 5.Nxe5 {White captures one of our pawns and gets out of danger. Expect to see this move by far the most, 74% of the time. Let's take on e5! White would've been willing to take us on c6 either way. Now the bishop on b5 is misplaced, surely.} 5...Nxe5 6.dxe5 {Recapturing. By playing c6, we gain a tempo on the bishop! Additionally, we open up the diagonal so our queen can check on a5, followed by taking on e5!} 6...c6 7.Nc3 {Craziness on the board! The bishop is hanging! But White argues they have counterplay. Only 14% of players will know about this tricky move. We'll take the bishop.} 7...cxb5 8.Nxe4 {White captures our pawn in the center. Careful! There are some lines open to our king! With ...d5, we give up a pawn but facilitate our development.} 8...d5 9.exd6 {En passant! White is obviously a cultured individual. We see an immediate benefit of giving up our d-pawn, and can now develop the knight to f6.} 9...Nf6 10.Bg5 {Another idea is for White to pin our knight, attacking it with two pieces. We'll give a check and unpin with tempo!} 10...Qa5+ 11.Nc3 {The knight blocks. We'll push ...b4 and attack two pieces.} 11...b4 12.Bxf6 {White saves a piece by taking one. We'll recapture on f6.} 12...gxf6 13.Nd5 {The white knight leaps forward, threatening to come to c7. ...b3+ is our best move here.} 13...b3+ 14.c3 {c3 blocks. And now we go for maximum development!} 14...Be6 15.Nc7+ {White checks us. We'll protect the e6-bishop, and hide behind the d-pawn.} 15...Kd7 16.O-O {White can castle in hopes of bringing another attacker. We'll take the knight's defender, forcing the issue.} 16...Bxd6 17.Nxe6 {This capture is better as it draws our king out and takes away one of our good defenders. But we're not obligated to recapture! Instead, we can threaten mate on h2!} 17...Qe5 18.Re1 {We're basically allowing white to try to preserve their material because of the counterplay we're getting. We'll take on h2 with check.} 18...Qxh2+ 19.Kf1 {White steps away. We'll give another check to force the king out and then we will take on g2.} 19...Qh1+ 20.Ke2 {White moves out of the way.} 20...Qxg2 {We grab on g2. As you can see, the knight is still alive on e6, but White's king is doing terribly! Besides, our rooks will be coming to the e- and d-files. The rook coming to the d-file must be timed though, as there's a knight controlling that square.} *

