1.e4 {In this study we'll go over the position after 1.e4 e5 2.Nf3 Nc6 3.Bb5 f5 4.d4! You can mentally refer to this position as White counterattacks in the center. Let's begin! White opens with the king's pawn. We'll reply with ...e5.} 1...e5 2.Nf3 {The knight attacks e5. We'll defend the pawn with Nc6.} 2...Nc6 3.Bb5 {The Ruy Lopez, threatening the stability of our center! With ...f5, we enter our gambit!} 3...f5 4.d4 {White counters in the center! Notice each pawn in the center can be captured! This tricky move is played 5% of the time, which is around 80,000 games. Best is to capture on e4, destabilizing the knight on f3.} 4...fxe4 5.Nxe5 {White captures one of our pawns and gets out of danger. Expect to see this move by far the most, 74% of the time. Let's take on e5! White would've been willing to take us on c6 either way. Now the bishop on b5 is misplaced, surely.} 5...Nxe5 6.dxe5 {Recapturing. By playing c6, we gain a tempo on the bishop! Additionally, we open up the diagonal so our queen can check on a5, followed by taking on e5!} 6...c6 7.Nc3 {Craziness on the board! The bishop is hanging! But White argues they have counterplay. Only 14% of players will know about this tricky move. We'll take the bishop.} 7...cxb5 8.Nxe4 {White captures our pawn in the center. Careful! There are some lines open to our king! With ...d5, we give up a pawn but facilitate our development.} 8...d5 9.exd6 {En passant! White is obviously a cultured individual. We see an immediate benefit of giving up our d-pawn, and can now develop the knight to f6.} 9...Nf6 10.Qd4 {The queen is brought to pressurize f6, though this isn't the best move. Let's remove one of the attackers!} 10...Nxe4 11.Qxe4+ {White recaptures, giving check. Luckily our king can come out and be perfectly fine. He'll be safe on the light squares! Notice there's only one attacker at the moment, the queen. This is less dangerous than being attacked by many pieces!} 11...Kf7 12.Bf4 {Again, White may recognize one attacker isn't enough, so a second one is brought in. (But it's not fast enough, and the bishop doesn't coordinate well with the queen). We propose a queen trade, as we pin the queen to the king, and we place ourselves on a light square to defend that color complex.} 12...Qe8 13.Be5 {The bishop interposes, blocking the pin and the trade proposal.} 13...Qc6 {Trade! Once more! After any check, we'll be safe on g8. Just don't undefend the light squares!} *

