1.e4 {In this study we'll go over the position after 1.e4 e5 2.Nf3 Nc6 3.Bb5 f5 4.d4! You can mentally refer to this position as White counterattacks in the center. Let's begin! White opens with the king's pawn. We'll reply with ...e5.} 1...e5 2.Nf3 {The knight attacks e5. We'll defend the pawn with Nc6.} 2...Nc6 3.Bb5 {The Ruy Lopez, threatening the stability of our center! With ...f5, we enter our gambit!} 3...f5 4.d4 {White counters in the center! Notice each pawn in the center can be captured! This tricky move is played 5% of the time, which is around 80,000 games. Best is to capture on e4, destabilizing the knight on f3.} 4...fxe4 5.Nxe5 {White captures one of our pawns and gets out of danger. Expect to see this move by far the most, 74% of the time. Let's take on e5! White would've been willing to take us on c6 either way. Now the bishop on b5 is misplaced, surely.} 5...Nxe5 6.dxe5 {Recapturing. By playing c6, we gain a tempo on the bishop! Additionally, we open up the diagonal so our queen can check on a5, followed by taking on e5!} 6...c6 7.Nc3 {Craziness on the board! The bishop is hanging! But White argues they have counterplay. Only 14% of players will know about this tricky move. We'll take the bishop.} 7...cxb5 8.Nxe4 {White captures our pawn in the center. Careful! There are some lines open to our king! With ...d5, we give up a pawn but facilitate our development.} 8...d5 9.exd6 {En passant! White is obviously a cultured individual. We see an immediate benefit of giving up our d-pawn, and can now develop the knight to f6.} 9...Nf6 10.Qd4 {The queen is brought to pressurize f6, though this isn't the best move. Let's remove one of the attackers!} 10...Nxe4 11.Qxe4+ {White recaptures, giving check. Luckily our king can come out and be perfectly fine. He'll be safe on the light squares! Notice there's only one attacker at the moment, the queen. This is less dangerous than being attacked by many pieces!} 11...Kf7 12.Qd5+ {White may continue trying to harass us. The king can safely come upwards. White doesn't have enough active pieces to mount a dangerous attack.} 12...Kg6 13.Bf4 {The other attacker joins a little too late. We'll check with our queen.} 13...Qe8+ 14.Be5 {White blocks it. We'll propose a trade of queens!} 14...Qc6 15.Qd4 {White obviously rejects it. But we'll keep trying to trade!} 15...Qc4 16.Qe3 {White refuses yet again!} 16...Bf5 {And we develop another piece! We might propose a trade that cannot be denied next move.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Jaenisch Gambit Sidelines / Study 2: White Plays 4.d4 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/0aa60d8d-e4ca-448a-808c-630cb0656846/chapters/08487333-b01d-45ad-9441-10369ea04ed5/studies/b55f0438-0a0f-4ef5-a694-f49390549d42/lines"]

1.e4 {In this study we'll go over the position after 1.e4 e5 2.Nf3 Nc6 3.Bb5 f5 4.d4! You can mentally refer to this position as White counterattacks in the center. Let's begin! White opens with the king's pawn. We'll reply with ...e5.} 1...e5 2.Nf3 {The knight attacks e5. We'll defend the pawn with Nc6.} 2...Nc6 3.Bb5 {The Ruy Lopez, threatening the stability of our center! With ...f5, we enter our gambit!} 3...f5 4.d4 {White counters in the center! Notice each pawn in the center can be captured! This tricky move is played 5% of the time, which is around 80,000 games. Best is to capture on e4, destabilizing the knight on f3.} 4...fxe4 5.Nxe5 {White captures one of our pawns and gets out of danger. Expect to see this move by far the most, 74% of the time. Let's take on e5! White would've been willing to take us on c6 either way. Now the bishop on b5 is misplaced, surely.} 5...Nxe5 6.dxe5 {Recapturing. By playing c6, we gain a tempo on the bishop! Additionally, we open up the diagonal so our queen can check on a5, followed by taking on e5!} 6...c6 7.Nc3 {Craziness on the board! The bishop is hanging! But White argues they have counterplay. Only 14% of players will know about this tricky move. We'll take the bishop.} 7...cxb5 8.Nxe4 {White captures our pawn in the center. Careful! There are some lines open to our king! With ...d5, we give up a pawn but facilitate our development.} 8...d5 9.exd6 {En passant! White is obviously a cultured individual. We see an immediate benefit of giving up our d-pawn, and can now develop the knight to f6.} 9...Nf6 10.O-O {White castles, with the idea of bringing a rook into the attack. We'll take on e4, but our king is very open and we'll need to be precise in our play.} 10...Nxe4 11.Qh5+ {Check! Here we block with the pawn. We're ultimately losing the rook on h8, but the alternative is ...Kd7 Qxb5+ and our king gets drawn into the center of the board.} 11...g6 12.Qe5+ {Check! Forking our pieces. Let's bring the king up, away from the e-file and into a safer position.} 12...Kf7 13.Qxh8 {White is advised to take the most valuable piece. Our h-pawn is under attack. Thus we fall back with Nf6 and defend the pawn.} 13...Nf6 14.Bh6 {White applies pressure on our pinned bishop, not least because their queen has no squares. But now we can just develop to e6 and our queen is defended by the rook.} 14...Be6 15.Bxf8 {Thus trading is the best they can do. And we'll recapture.} 15...Qxf8 16.Qxf8+ {White is compelled to take.} 16...Rxf8 {And we recapture, with a favorable ending. We have two pieces for the rook. Our rook will come to d8 and our knight to d5. We'll be winning the advanced d-pawn shortly.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Jaenisch Gambit Sidelines / Study 2: White Plays 4.d4 / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/0aa60d8d-e4ca-448a-808c-630cb0656846/chapters/08487333-b01d-45ad-9441-10369ea04ed5/studies/b55f0438-0a0f-4ef5-a694-f49390549d42/lines"]

1.e4 {In this study we'll go over the position after 1.e4 e5 2.Nf3 Nc6 3.Bb5 f5 4.d4! You can mentally refer to this position as White counterattacks in the center. Let's begin! White opens with the king's pawn. We'll reply with ...e5.} 1...e5 2.Nf3 {The knight attacks e5. We'll defend the pawn with Nc6.} 2...Nc6 3.Bb5 {The Ruy Lopez, threatening the stability of our center! With ...f5, we enter our gambit!} 3...f5 4.d4 {White counters in the center! Notice each pawn in the center can be captured! This tricky move is played 5% of the time, which is around 80,000 games. Best is to capture on e4, destabilizing the knight on f3.} 4...fxe4 5.Bxc6 {Alternatively, White can diminish our central defense by taking off the knight. This is played 22% of the time. Capturing away from the center unleashes both queen and light-squared bishop!} 5...dxc6 6.Nxe5 {The knight gets out of danger. We'll develop, supporting e4.} 6...Nf6 7.Nc3 {Nc3 applies pressure on e4. We'll develop to d6, pressurizing the knight.} 7...Bd6 8.Bg5 {The bishop pins our knight. We'll get our king to safety and support our knight with the rook.} 8...O-O 9.Qe2 {Another piece adds pressure to e4.} 9...Qe8 {We can get out of the pin and pressure e5. Next, developing moves like ...Bf5 and ...Qg6 are great if the knight moves, to get some pieces to the kingside. Centralizing the a-rook is always a good idea.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Jaenisch Gambit Sidelines / Study 2: White Plays 4.d4 / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/0aa60d8d-e4ca-448a-808c-630cb0656846/chapters/08487333-b01d-45ad-9441-10369ea04ed5/studies/b55f0438-0a0f-4ef5-a694-f49390549d42/lines"]

1.e4 {In this study we'll go over the position after 1.e4 e5 2.Nf3 Nc6 3.Bb5 f5 4.d4! You can mentally refer to this position as White counterattacks in the center. Let's begin! White opens with the king's pawn. We'll reply with ...e5.} 1...e5 2.Nf3 {The knight attacks e5. We'll defend the pawn with Nc6.} 2...Nc6 3.Bb5 {The Ruy Lopez, threatening the stability of our center! With ...f5, we enter our gambit!} 3...f5 4.d4 {White counters in the center! Notice each pawn in the center can be captured! This tricky move is played 5% of the time, which is around 80,000 games. Best is to capture on e4, destabilizing the knight on f3.} 4...fxe4 5.Nxe5 {White captures one of our pawns and gets out of danger. Expect to see this move by far the most, 74% of the time. Let's take on e5! White would've been willing to take us on c6 either way. Now the bishop on b5 is misplaced, surely.} 5...Nxe5 6.dxe5 {Recapturing. By playing c6, we gain a tempo on the bishop! Additionally, we open up the diagonal so our queen can check on a5, followed by taking on e5!} 6...c6 7.Bc4 {This simple retreats lets us have what we want. And it's played 61% of the time!} 7...Qa5+ {This check is a fork! We'll take on e5 and play d5, with an extra pawn and a strong center.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Jaenisch Gambit Sidelines / Study 3: White Plays 4.Qe2 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/0aa60d8d-e4ca-448a-808c-630cb0656846/chapters/08487333-b01d-45ad-9441-10369ea04ed5/studies/d25343cb-23c7-4ce1-91b3-b1df2e00d564/lines"]

1.e4 {This study is pretty straightforward. We reach our standard gambit opening with 1.e4 e5 2. f3 Nc6 3.Bb5 f5, and white defends e4 with 4.Qe2. You'll achieve equality pretty quickly in this line, so there's no need to worry! Let's begin! White opens with the king's pawn. We'll reply with ...e5.} 1...e5 2.Nf3 {The knight attacks e5. We'll defend the pawn with Nc6.} 2...Nc6 3.Bb5 {The Ruy Lopez, threatening the stability of our center! With ...f5, we enter our gambit!} 3...f5 4.Qe2 {White defends e4 with the queen. This move is only played 1% of the time, in around 12,000 games, but that doesn't mean you won't come across it! At this juncture, I suggest capturing.} 4...fxe4 5.Qxe4 {White recaptures with the queen as originally intended. We can develop with a tempo on the queen.} 5...Nf6 6.Qe2 {The queen goes back. We'll develop, supporting e5.} 6...Bd6 7.d4 {White pushes, attacking our center, and trying to exploit the pin on the e-file. We'll push the e-pawn and eye the f3-knight.} 7...e4 8.c3 {White consolidates d4. But now we can unpin our e-pawn by repositioning our bishop to e7!} 8...Be7 9.Ne5 {White's knight was under attack so they decide to jump into a central location.} 9...O-O {And here we can castle safely, with ideas of d7-d5 consolidating our pawn on e4, after the pressure on c6 is resolved. If the knights are traded, we can get a piece to the g4-square.} *